1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,663
Look up on a clear day
and the sky is blue.

2
00:00:02,663 --> 00:00:04,175
But sunlight is white.

3
00:00:04,175 --> 00:00:05,904
So where does the blue come from?

4
00:00:06,354 --> 00:00:08,316
Wait — sunlight is white?

5
00:00:08,316 --> 00:00:09,854
It looks yellow to me.

6
00:00:10,254 --> 00:00:13,129
Sunlight contains every
colour of the rainbow.

7
00:00:13,129 --> 00:00:17,429
When it enters the atmosphere,
it bumps into tiny gas molecules.

8
00:00:17,429 --> 00:00:21,796
Short, blue wavelengths scatter
much more than long, red ones.

9
00:00:23,046 --> 00:00:28,226
So blue light bounces all over the sky, and
that's what we see from every direction!

10
00:00:28,626 --> 00:00:29,526
Exactly.

11
00:00:29,526 --> 00:00:32,775
And at sunset, light
travels through more air,

12
00:00:32,775 --> 00:00:36,784
so the blue is scattered away
— leaving reds and oranges.

13
00:00:37,234 --> 00:00:39,966
Subscribe for more one-minute science.
